<title>USA: State Farm says Fran losses won't rival Hugo.</title>
<headline>State Farm says Fran losses won't rival Hugo.</headline>
<dateline>BLOOMINGTON, Ill. 1996-09-06</dateline>
<text>
<p>State Farm Insurance on Friday said initial indications are that property damage claims from Hurricane Fran will not rival Hurricane Hugo.</p>
<p>The 1989 storm had resulted in State Farm paying $455 million in claims that numbered more than 127,000.</p>
<p>State Farm has 434,000 homeowners policies and 875,000 auto policies in North Carolina, which bore the brunt of Hurricane Fran.</p>
<p>Hugo was the company's third most costly catastrophe in terms of claim costs. Hurricane Andrew, which hit south Florida in 1992, cost nearly $3.7 billion. The Northbridge, Calif. earthquake in 1994 was the second most costly at $2.9 billion.</p>
<p>State Farm identifies itself as the nation's largest insurer of homes and cars.</p>
<p>	   -- Chicago newsdesk 312 408-8787</p>
</text>
